"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[43724],{943724:e=>{e.exports="# Performance Tuning — Clustering, Caching, Statistics\n\nSnowflake performance features and their DBSQL equivalents or lack thereof.\n\n## What to drop unconditionally\n\nThese Snowflake features have no equivalent in DBSQL and must be removed without substitution:\n\n| Snowflake                                                             | Remove                                                                                          |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `WAREHOUSE = my_wh` clauses in `CREATE TASK` / `CREATE PIPE`          | DBSQL compute is configured at the job/task level, not in SQL                                   |\n| `QUERY_TAG` session setting (`ALTER SESSION SET QUERY_TAG = ...`)     | No equivalent — use Workflow job labels / tags                                                  |\n| Search Optimization Service (`ALTER TABLE t ADD SEARCH OPTIMIZATION`) | No direct equivalent — DBSQL uses data skipping, Liquid Clustering, Z-ORDER, and predictive I/O |\n| `AUTOMATIC_CLUSTERING` on/off toggles                                 | DBSQL Liquid Clustering is always on per-table                                                  |\n| `RESULT_SCAN(LAST_QUERY_ID())`                                        | No equivalent — DBSQL caches automatically, no query-id re-access                               |\n| Snowflake micro-partition pruning hints                               | Not user-visible in DBSQL; handled automatically                                                |\n| `MAX_CONCURRENCY_LEVEL` / warehouse auto-scale params                 | DBSQL SQL warehouse scaling configured outside SQL                                              |\n\n## What to translate\n\n| Snowflake                                                           | DBSQL equivalent                                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CLUSTER BY (col)` clustering keys                                  | `CLUSTER BY (col)` (Liquid Clustering — GA on DBR 15.2+; write-only preview on DBR 13.3+) |\n| Manual recluster (`ALTER TABLE t RECLUSTER`)                        | `OPTIMIZE my_catalog.my_schema.t` + Liquid Clustering auto-optimizes on write             |\n| Materialized views                                                  | Materialized views in DBSQL (refresh semantics differ)                                    |\n| Partitioned tables (Snowflake doesn't expose partitioning directly) | `PARTITIONED BY (date_col)` — for low-cardinality cols; `CLUSTER BY` for high-cardinality |\n| Micro-partitions (automatic)                                        | Delta handles file-level statistics automatically                                         |\n| Columnstore (always on)                                             | Delta is columnar by default                                                              |\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### Clustering keys\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE TABLE fact_sales (region VARCHAR, order_date DATE, amount NUMBER)\nCLUSTER BY (region, order_date);\n\n-- Redefine clustering key\nALTER TABLE fact_sales CLUSTER BY (region, customer_id);\n\n-- Drop clustering\nALTER TABLE fact_sales DROP CLUSTERING KEY;\n\n-- DBSQL (Liquid Clustering — recommended)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales (region STRING, order_date DATE, amount DECIMAL(18,2))\nCLUSTER BY (region, order_date);\n\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales CLUSTER BY (region, customer_id);\n\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales CLUSTER BY NONE;\n\n-- DBSQL alternative for legacy / Hive-partitioned layouts: PARTITIONED BY + ZORDER\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales (region STRING, order_date DATE, amount DECIMAL(18,2))\nPARTITIONED BY (order_date);\nOPTIMIZE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales ZORDER BY (region);\n```\n\n**Notes:**\n\n- Snowflake auto-reclusters in the background (charged). DBSQL Liquid Clustering optimizes on write and via explicit `OPTIMIZE`.\n- Liquid Clustering supports altering the clustering keys in place; Snowflake also supports this.\n- Z-ORDER is legacy; prefer Liquid Clustering on new/converted tables.\n- Choosing: Liquid Clustering for multi-dimensional access patterns and high-cardinality columns; `PARTITIONED BY` for low-cardinality date/region columns where partition pruning helps; Z-ORDER only when migrating an existing partition + Z-ORDER layout.\n\n### Search Optimization Service\n\nSnowflake SOS adds a secondary access structure for selective predicates on high-cardinality columns (e.g. `WHERE transaction_id = 'abc123'`). DBSQL has no direct equivalent. Near-equivalents:\n\n- **Data skipping** (automatic in Delta, min/max per file) — always on\n- **Z-ORDER BY (col)** (legacy optimization for clustered point lookups)\n- **Liquid Clustering** (preferred) — similar effect on chosen columns\n- **Predictive I/O** (Photon-enabled compute, DBR 12.2+) automatically performs file skipping on all columns — often the closest equivalent in practice.\n\nCombine Liquid Clustering on the access-pattern column with data skipping to approximate SOS behavior. Benchmark after migration — predictive I/O on Photon often matches or exceeds SOS performance without user action.\n\n> Bloom filter indexes (`CREATE BLOOMFILTER INDEX`) are **deprecated** in Databricks — do not introduce them in new code. If the source codebase has existing Bloom filter indexes, drop them via `DROP BLOOMFILTER INDEX ON TABLE ...`.\n\n### Materialized views\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW sales_by_region AS\nSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY region;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.sales_by_region AS\nSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders GROUP BY region;\n\n-- Refresh explicitly or on schedule\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.sales_by_region;\n```\n\n**Differences:**\n\n- Snowflake MVs auto-maintain against base table changes (background, charged). DBSQL MVs refresh on manual trigger, schedule, or incrementally within a DLT pipeline.\n- DBSQL MV restrictions: source tables must be Delta; limited SQL constructs supported (no `MATCH_RECOGNIZE`, some UDF/UDAF restrictions).\n- If the source depends on near-real-time freshness, consider a DLT streaming table / materialized view in a DLT pipeline (see `references/snowflake/07-pipelines.md`).\n\n### Query hints\n\nSnowflake has very few query hints — most tuning is automatic. If the Snowflake source has no hints (typical), do not add DBSQL hints during migration.\n\nDBSQL hints (add only when the original Snowflake code had explicit tuning):\n\n```sql\n-- Broadcast a small table\nSELECT /*+ BROADCAST(small_table) */ ... FROM big_table JOIN small_table ON ...\n\n-- Control parallelism / repartition\nSELECT /*+ COALESCE(4) */ ...\nSELECT /*+ REPARTITION(8, region) */ ...\n```\n\n### QUERY_TAG\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nALTER SESSION SET QUERY_TAG = 'etl_pipeline_orders';\n\n-- DBSQL: no SQL-level equivalent. Use platform-level tagging:\n-- - Workflow task tags / labels\n-- - Job run parameters\n-- - Programmatic session setting in a notebook Python cell:\n--     spark.conf.set(\"spark.databricks.labels.pipeline\", \"etl_orders\")\n```\n\nIf the Snowflake source uses `QUERY_TAG` for billing / usage attribution, replicate via Workflow tags so usage shows up in Databricks `system.billing.usage`.\n\n### Warehouse sizing and auto-suspend\n\nSnowflake:\n\n```sql\nCREATE WAREHOUSE my_wh\nWAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'LARGE'\nAUTO_SUSPEND = 60\nAUTO_RESUME = TRUE;\n```\n\nDBSQL SQL Warehouse config — Size, Auto Stop minutes, Serverless option — is configured in the Databricks UI or via the `databricks` CLI / Terraform provider. Not expressed in SQL. Drop these DDL statements during migration; move to infra code.\n\n### Result caching\n\n- **Snowflake result cache**: 24-hour retention, keyed by exact query text + role. Subsequent identical query returns cached result for free.\n- **DBSQL**: Disk cache (Databricks cache) on warm SQL warehouses + Photon result cache (automatic). No user-visible query id lookup.\n- `RESULT_SCAN(query_id)` — no DBSQL equivalent. Re-run the query or materialize results in a table if needed.\n\n### OPTIMIZE and VACUUM (DBSQL maintenance)\n\nSnowflake handles file management automatically. DBSQL exposes two user-facing maintenance commands:\n\n```sql\n-- Compact small files, optionally re-cluster\nOPTIMIZE my_catalog.my_schema.t;\nOPTIMIZE my_catalog.my_schema.t ZORDER BY (col1, col2);\n\n-- Reclaim storage for deleted rows / old file versions\n-- (respects delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration; default 7 days)\nVACUUM my_catalog.my_schema.t;\n```\n\nAfter migration, schedule `OPTIMIZE` + `VACUUM` in a recurring Workflow task (daily or weekly, depending on write volume). With Liquid Clustering, `OPTIMIZE` also applies clustering.\n\n### Automatic statistics\n\nBoth dialects collect table statistics automatically for the optimizer; no user action required. Neither dialect exposes a manual `RECOMPUTE STATISTICS` / `UPDATE STATISTICS` command — there is nothing to translate.\n"}}]);
